妇女拘留所的女性距离大约500英尺。她们能听到发生了什么。她们之前曾暴动过，她们知道怎么做，对吧？她们点燃了自己的财物。这些小窗户，这些贝利窗户，刚好够你把手伸出去，你可以向下面的人扔东西。休实际上在口述历史中听到了对此的描述，

49
00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:33.000
Arcus was driving home through the village, east on Greenwich Avenue, when she saw the women inside the house of detention joining in with the people on the streets below. I saw all these tiny little fires coming out of the women's house of detention. They had lit cigarette boxes and cellophane from the cigarette packs
来自一位名叫阿库斯·弗林的女性。阿库斯开车穿过村子回家，在格林威治大道向东行驶时，她看到拘留所里的女性加入了下面街上的人群。我看到所有这些微小的火光从妇女拘留所里冒出来。她们点燃了香烟盒和香烟包装纸上的玻璃纸，

50
00:27:33.000 --> 00:28:05.000
and whatever books, and they had dropped them out through the bars and the window. Then I stopped the car. car and parked and tried to listen to what the women were saying. They were screaming, gay rights, gay rights, gay rights, gay rights. The Stonewall uprising lasts six days. And when it's over, the crowd doesn't really disperse. It converts. All that energy moves indoors into meetings, full of people who weren't activists a week ago, sitting across from people who've been organizing
以及任何书本，她们把它们从栏杆和窗户扔了出去。然后我停下车，停好车，试图听听女人们在说什么。她们在尖叫：“同性恋权利，同性恋权利，同性恋权利，同性恋权利。”石墙起义持续了六天。当它结束时，人群并没有真正散去。它转化了。所有的能量都转移到了室内，进入了会议中，里面坐满了一周前还不是活动家的人，与那些已经安静地组织了

51
00:28:05.000 --> 00:28:42.000
quietly for 15 years. And the question on the table is where to point the energy next. And they say, we want to protest the prison in support of the Black Panthers who are incarcerated there. And the older activists say, don't do anything that will piss off the cops. We don't support this. And the younger activists immediately walk out of the meeting and they announce themselves to be the Gay Liberation Front, a name they've taken from the Vietnamese Liberation Front, the Communist Organization. And their first action is to protest the Women's House of Detention. Historians have called the Gay Liberation Front, or the GLF, the incubator of the modern
15年的人坐在一起。桌上的问题是接下来要把能量指向哪里。她们说，我们想抗议监狱，以支持被关押在那里的黑豹党人。年长的活动家说，不要做任何会惹恼警察的事情。我们不支持这个。年轻的活动家立即走出会议室，宣布自己是“同性恋解放阵线”，这个名字取自越南解放阵线，即共产主义组织。她们的第一个行动就是抗议妇女拘留所。历史学家称“同性恋解放阵线”或GLF为现代

52
00:28:42.000 --> 00:29:29.000
gay rights movement. And the thing that started at all was a prison, the women's house of detention. In fact, the GOF, along with a half dozen other groups, were behind what became known as the first Pride March held on the anniversary of the Stonewall riots. And they kept protesting the House of D. They held rallies, set up a round-the-clock vigil that ran for a week. At one protest, one of the marchers brought his own recorder to capture it. A public radio reporter and one of the founders of the GLF, Charles Pitt. We're passing the women's house of detention. You see that in many of the windows, there are forms.
同性恋权利运动的孵化器。而这一切的开始是一座监狱，妇女拘留所。事实上，GLF与其他六个团体一起，是后来被称为在石墙骚乱周年纪念日举行的第一次骄傲游行的幕后推手。她们继续抗议D之家。她们举行集会，设立了持续一周的24小时守夜活动。在一次抗议中，其中一名游行者带了自己的录音机来记录。一位公共广播记者和GLF的创始人之一，查尔斯·皮特。我们经过妇女拘留所。你看到在许多窗户里，有身影。

53
00:29:29.000 --> 00:30:02.000
The windows are dirty. But there are people waving from the windows. The chant that they were chanting, which I didn't understand, was free our sisters in the house of D. Okay, so I want to step back because I don't know exactly how to ask this. but I want you to think through this with me. So this place, the House of D, it targets queer women in particular, and a prison is not a great place. But it's also this place where the women find themselves. They understand their sexuality more.
窗户很脏。但窗户里有人在挥手。她们喊的口号，我没听懂，是“释放我们在D之家的姐妹”。好吧，我想退后一步，因为我不知道该怎么问这个问题，但我希望你和我一起思考。所以这个地方，D之家，它特别针对酷儿女性，监狱不是一个好地方。但它也是这些女性找到自己的地方。她们更了解自己的性取向。

54
00:30:02.000 --> 00:30:34.000
You know, like they're taught about what community they could belong in, what they could be a part of, what families they can form. I mean, these are really hard things to reconcile, the goodness and the badness that's mixed in with all this sort of stuff. So how do you tease that apart? You know, I talked to a lot of women who were incarcerated there. J-Tool, incredible activist. She talked to me a lot about her times at the House of Detention. And at one point she said to me, a lot of us called it the prison, but a lot of us called it the playground. It was where you met other queer women.
你知道，就像她们被教导可以属于什么社区，可以成为什么的一部分，可以组成什么样的家庭。我的意思是，这些真的很难调和，混杂在所有这些事情中的善与恶。所以你如何把它们分开？你知道，我和很多被关在那里的女性谈过。J-Tool，了不起的活动家。她和我谈了很多关于她在拘留所的时光。有一次她对我说，我们很多人称它为监狱，但我们很多人称它为游乐场。那是你遇见其他酷儿女性的地方。

55
00:30:34.000 --> 00:31:10.000
And so many of these women not only met each other there, but would tell stories about how, you know, they had never really thought about the idea of desiring another woman until they were in the prison. and they met women who had had relationships with other women. I think so much of queer history, queer life in America, certainly pre-21st century, is about the repurposing of things that were not meant for us, right? What is camp? What is the taking up of, you know, heterosexual melodramas? What is drag?
许多女性不仅在那里相识，还会讲述她们的故事，比如，你知道，在入狱之前，她们从未真正想过渴望另一个女性。直到她们进了监狱，遇到了那些曾与女性有过关系的女性。我认为美国酷儿历史和酷儿生活中的很大一部分，当然是在21世纪之前，都是关于对那些本不属于我们的东西进行重新利用，对吧？什么是坎普风（camp）？什么是对异性恋情节剧的挪用？什么是变装？

56
00:31:10.000 --> 00:31:46.000
So much of this is about taking a world that we are forced to exist inside and saying, What can we make of this our own? People, no matter where you put us, we want to live, we want to create, we want to laugh, we want to yell. And if all we're given to do that through is a prison, yeah, we'll do it there, right? You can't legislate us out of existence, you can't even put us in cages, you kill us, and it won't matter. The saving grace and the fatal flaw of straight people is that they make more queer people, right? And so these kind of attempts inevitably fail.
这很大程度上是关于如何在一个我们被迫生存的世界里，说：我们能从中创造出属于我们自己的东西吗？无论你把我们放在哪里，人们都想要生活，想要创造，想要欢笑，想要呐喊。如果监狱是我们实现这些的唯一途径，是的，我们也会在那里做，对吧？你无法通过立法让我们消失，你甚至把我们关进笼子里，你杀了我们，那也无济于事。异性恋者的救赎与致命缺陷在于，他们会生出更多的酷儿，对吧？所以这些尝试注定会失败。

57
00:31:46.000 --> 00:32:36.000
In 1971, the House of D shuddered. The complaints from inside had gotten too loud, the abuses too egregious to ignore. The women quietly were bused out to a new institution on Rikers Island. Much like the House of D in the announcements about this new institution, it's going to be bigger, brighter, nicer, nicer, kinder. It's going to have a room for women who are pregnant and a nursery for children, all of which is stripped out before, of course, it opens. I mean, it is just as bad as the House of Detention. mention and worse. And now it's on an island. It's impossible to get there. It takes two hours. It costs a lot of money. And all of these incarcerated people who at least before were able to be
1971年，女子拘留所（House of D）关闭了。内部的投诉声太大了，虐待行为也太恶劣，无法忽视。女性们被悄悄地用巴士转移到了赖克斯岛（Rikers Island）的一所新机构。就像关于这个新机构的公告中所说的那样，它会更大、更明亮、更好、更友善。它会为怀孕的女性提供房间，为孩子提供托儿所，当然，在开放之前，所有这些设施都被拆除了。我的意思是，它和女子拘留所一样糟糕，甚至更糟。而且现在它位于一座岛上。去那里是不可能的。需要两个小时。花费很多钱。所有这些被监禁的人，至少在以前还能被

58
00:32:36.000 --> 00:33:26.000
visited are now truly isolated. And that, of course, is the goal, right? We don't want this kind of communication between the incarcerated and the non-incarcerated. The House of D closed right as the country descended into an age of mass incarceration. In the early 1970s, the U.S. had around 300,000 people locked up. Within 30 years, it was close to 2 million. All those people had to go somewhere, and it wasn't cities. Through the 1990s, a new prison opened somewhere in rural America once about every 15 days. And that's where most people in state and federal prison are now. Emptied farm country, shuttered mill towns, prisons built over old coal minds. Now, the windows don't open, and even if they did, you'd be shouting into an empty field.
探视，现在却真正被孤立了。当然，这就是目的，对吧？我们不希望被监禁者和非被监禁者之间有这种交流。女子拘留所关闭时，正值全国陷入大规模监禁时代。在20世纪70年代初，美国大约有30万人被关押。30年内，这个数字接近200万。所有这些人必须去某个地方，而那不是城市。在整个20世纪90年代，美国农村地区大约每15天就会开设一所新监狱。这就是现在大多数州立和联邦监狱囚犯所在的地方。空旷的农田、关闭的磨坊小镇、建在旧煤矿上的监狱。现在，窗户打不开，即使打开了，你也只是对着空旷的田野大喊大叫。

59
00:33:26.000 --> 00:34:14.000
When I think about the lessons we can learn from the House of Detention, it goes back to those windows, right? The windows were a moment of communication, of openness, of space, and they make me ask this question. I think the essential question we should be asking today, anytime we do any story on prisons. Why do we think that detention is the right method for dealing with social distortion? What is it about putting someone in a cage away from their community, their family, their jobs, education, the son, that we think will improve something? The windows on the house of detention feel like this very last moment where the judicial system, the legal system in America, was asking that question.
当我思考我们可以从女子拘留所学到的教训时，我会回到那些窗户，对吧？窗户是一个交流、开放和空间的时刻，它们让我问出这个问题。我认为这是我们今天在做任何关于监狱的报道时都应该问的核心问题。为什么我们认为拘留是处理社会扭曲的正确方法？把一个人关在远离他们的社区、家庭、工作、教育和儿子的笼子里，我们认为这能改善什么？女子拘留所的窗户感觉像是美国司法系统、法律系统在问那个问题的最后一个时刻。

60
00:34:14.000 --> 00:35:04.000
The House of Dee, sitting on a bustling corner of Granch Village, couldn't be ignored, and its presence had an impact on the people around it. I had never thought about women in prison. Including Audra Lord, who spent years walking past it. And then a prison in the middle of where I'm living, it brought home to me a kind of a very essential reality about prisons and about women in prison, a connectedness that I would not have gotten any other place. I think many of us just would never have thought of. When the House of D came down, it took that with it, not just the women, the fact of them.
女子拘留所坐落在格林威治村（Greenwich Village）繁华的街角，无法被忽视，它的存在对周围的人产生了影响。我从未想过监狱里的女性。包括奥德丽·洛德（Audra Lord），她多年来一直从那里走过。然后，在我居住的地方中间出现了一座监狱，它让我深刻体会到关于监狱和监狱中女性的一种非常本质的现实，一种我在其他任何地方都无法获得的联系感。我想我们许多人根本就不会想到这一点。当女子拘留所倒塌时，它带走了这一切，不仅仅是那些女性，还有她们存在的事实。

61
00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:42.000
